# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
opp	one
gen	one
souls_below	one
# opp: P1 is the general claim and P2/P3 apply it to cases. The text treats life/death as opposites exactly as it treats sleep/waking.
# Some may object that life/death (either-or) are not opposites like greater/less (which pass through intermediate degrees). That bears on whether P1 is true, not on what "opposite" means, since the text uses the word one way throughout.
# gen: "generated out of" means, everywhere, that a thing passes from one state into its opposite state. S39 applies this to "things or persons", and A1 reads it the same way: if the living come from the dead, the dead must exist somewhere to come back from.
# A1 joins two different claims, each in its own sense, so it is not a bridge.
